      "text": "To determine the number of different settings on a suitcase lock that has three dials with digits from \\(0\\) to \\(9\\) where all three digits must be different, we need to follow a step-by-step approach. Each digit can be any one of the 10 digits (0 through 9).\n\n1. **First dial**: The first dial can show any of the 10 digits. So there are 10 possible choices for the first dial.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Choices for the first dial} = 10\n   \\]\n\n2. **Second dial**: The second dial can show any of the remaining 9 digits (since the digit cannot repeat the digit on the first dial).\n   \\[\n   \\text{Choices for the second dial} = 9\n   \\]\n\n3. **Third dial**: The third dial can show any of the remaining 8 digits (since the digits cannot repeat on any of the three dials).\n   \\[\n   \\text{Choices for the third dial} = 8\n   \\]\n\nTo find the total number of different settings, we multiply the number of choices for each dial:\n\\[\n10 \\times 9 \\times 8\n\\]\n\nNow, we perform the multiplication:\n\\[\n10 \\times 9 = 90\n\\]\n\\[\n90 \\times 8 = 720\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of different settings possible is \\(\\boxed{720}\\).",
